## Why This Is Asked
Combinatorial DP with constrained block lengths and non-adjacency tests careful state design, transitions, and reconstruction. It mirrors real problems where resources (disjoint intervals) have length bounds and spacing requirements.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefix index and blocks used
- Handling length bounds [L, R] in transitions
- Enforcing a gap between blocks (non-adjacency) and backpointer reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, L, R) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const take = Array.from({length:n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) {
    for (let t=0; t<=k; t++) dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], dp[i-1][t]);
    for (let len=L; len<=R; len++) {
      if (i-len < 0) break;
      if (t>0) {
        // placeholder: actual t loop omitted for brevity
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

